# Server-only backend origin. middleware.ts reads this when it proxies
# /api/backend/:path* to this host. It has no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, so
# Next.js does not inline it into the browser bundle.
#
# Local `next dev` and a local `next build` (VERCEL and VERCEL_ENV unset)
# use http://localhost:8000 when this variable is missing. Leave it unset
# for that. Do not put a real host, password, or API key in this file.
# Leave the line below commented. Copying this file to .env.local must
# not send local dev to the placeholder host.
#
# On Vercel, set it in the project environment variables:
#   Production — the production backend origin, for example https://api.example.com
#   Preview    — the same origin, or a staging origin if you have one
# A Production or Preview build fails when this is missing.
# Changing it requires a new deployment.

# BACKEND_API_URL=https://api.example.com

# Server-only secret. middleware.ts copies it onto every proxied request
# as x-sportslab-proxy-secret. Leave it unset locally: the proxy then
# forwards without that header. On a Vercel build it is required.
# Generate a value with `openssl rand -hex 32` and put the same value in
# the backend's VERCEL_PROXY_SECRET (AWS SSM; the backend ticket covers
# that side). Do not commit a real value. The line below is a placeholder.

# VERCEL_PROXY_SECRET=replace-with-openssl-rand-hex-32

# Vercel sets these on its own builds. Do not set them in local .env files.
# A non-empty VERCEL or VERCEL_ENV with no BACKEND_API_URL fails the build.
# VERCEL=
# VERCEL_ENV=
